<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basil Boioannes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 1017–1027</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basil Boioannes was the Byzantine catepan, or governor, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1017 to 1027. In 1018 he crushed the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melus-of-bari">Melus of Bari</a> and his Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> at the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a>. He strengthened Byzantine rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> by founding fortified towns such as Troia and extended imperial influence towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, making his governorship the high point of Byzantine power in southern Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basil-boioannes.jpg" alt="Basil Boioannes" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_Empire_Themes_1025-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
